+# Decision: Preview Cache Eviction Policy (2Q)
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker keeps a private, bounded cache of rendered JPEG contact sheets (12 GiB). The workload mixes two patterns: long sequential screenings that generate many one-use sheets, and comparison sessions that repeatedly revisit a small working set. The mix varies by production, so no single policy is best for every observed workload. Whatever policy is chosen must not change the external `GET /internal/previews/...` contract, the render queue's concurrency, or the 12 GiB budget (the review API holds no cache state and must not begin to). The policy determines whether the comparison working set survives a heavy screening; choosing wrongly means editors re-render hot comparison sheets at ~450 ms and 2 MiB per miss during exactly the sessions that can least afford it.
+
+This decision rests on the standing cache tenets: a cache is never the authoritative copy of footage, entries are keyed by immutable clip revision plus renderer version, a miss is ordinary behavior rather than an API failure, and cache paths and replacement bookkeeping stay private to the preview worker.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Gate (inherited): policy state stays private.** No policy state may leak outside the preview worker — no pinning requests, no eviction metadata exposed to the review API. All three options pass; it constrains their implementation, not their selection.
+- **Gate (inherited): budget and interface neutrality.** The option must enforce the 12 GiB bound and leave the three-outcome HTTP contract and four-job render concurrency unchanged. All three options pass.
+- **Scan resistance — major.** A long sequential screening (hundreds of one-use sheets) must not evict the small, repeatedly-hit comparison working set.
+- **Bookkeeping cost — moderate.** Per-hit and per-admission maintenance must stay cheap on the eight-core pilot worker.
+- **Adaptation to workload shifts — moderate.** When a production team switches projects, yesterday's popular clips should yield to the new project's working set without a long lag.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description:** A single queue in least-recently-used order. Every hit refreshes an entry to the most-recently-used end; admission enters at that end; eviction takes the least-recently-used end. Only recency order per entry is tracked.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Scan resistance: **weak** — a screening longer than the cache sequentially overwrites the entire cache, including the comparison working set.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong** — one recency move per hit, one list.
+- Adaptation: **strong** — recency tracks a project switch immediately.
+
+**Summary:** cheapest to run, most vulnerable to the workload pattern the archive actually sees.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description:** Each entry carries a hit counter; eviction takes the lowest count. Counts decay over time (aging) so yesterday's favorites do not dominate forever. Requires per-entry counters plus a decay mechanism.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — one-off screening sheets never accumulate enough hits to displace repeatedly popular clips.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak** — counter updates on every hit plus aging passes or decay arithmetic.
+- Adaptation: **partial** — aging reduces lag, but a decayed count still keeps a project-switched-away favorite resident longer than its actual reuse justifies.
+
+**Summary:** best retention of genuinely popular clips, at the cost of counter machinery and slow decay after a project switch.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description:** Two queues. New entries enter the first-use queue A1 (FIFO, ≤3 GiB); an entry requested a second time is promoted to the reused queue Am (LRU, ≤9 GiB), where subsequent hits refresh its position. Admission evicts from A1's FIFO tail while A1 is over its split, otherwise from Am's LRU tail. No ghost queue: an entry re-requested after eviction re-enters A1 as first-use.
+
+**Analysis:**
+- Scan resistance: **strong** — a screening populates and cycles only A1; comparison sheets, having been promoted to Am, are untouched by scan admissions.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial** — two queues and a promotion move on second access; still bounded, constant-time operations.
+- Adaptation: **strong** — entries unpromoted leave A1 within one pass; only genuinely reused sheets occupy Am.
+
+**Summary:** scan protection comparable to frequency counting without per-entry counters, paid for in two-queue bookkeeping and a split parameter to tune.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is selected. It is the only option strong on scan resistance without per-entry counter maintenance, and it adapts to project switches immediately since unpromoted entries cycle out of A1 within one screening. Frequency-with-aging matches its scan resistance but carries ongoing counter and decay cost and lags a project switch; LRU loses outright on scan resistance, the major criterion. The decision turns on the HLD tenets only through the gates, which all options pass — the selection is driven by workload fit, not by tenet conflict.
+
+Committing to 2Q obligates: (1) tuning the 3/9 GiB A1/Am split once real screening workloads are observed — the split is worker configuration, not interface, so retuning requires no cross-segment cascade; (2) keeping all queue state inside the worker, per the private-cache tenet; (3) accepting that Am is not absolutely sealed — with A1 filled exactly to its split, each admission draws one victim from Am, so scan protection is strong but not absolute.
+
+Implications for the rest of the design: the eviction mechanism is deliberately invisible at the review API segment — no pinning, no counters, no policy configuration crosses the internal HTTP boundary — so this decision does not create obligations in the review-api segment.
